  "verbalization": "Tolmetin, a nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drug (NSAID), is linked to Slc22a6 through a shared classification in the Nonsteroidal Anti-inflammatory Compounds (NSAIC) class. This connection is supported by multiple paths, all of which indicate that Tolmetin, along with its NSAID counterparts (Piroxicam, Etodolac, and Sulindac), binds to Slc22a6. The lowest common ancestors of these compounds—Organonitrogen Heterocyclic Compound and Nonsteroidal Antiinflammatory Drug—suggest a common pharmacological pathway. The evidence implies that the interaction between Tolmetin and Slc22a6 is mediated through a shared mechanism of action, where NSAIDs exert their effects by binding to Slc22a6, likely influencing transport or cellular function. The recurring association with NSAIDs and the consistent binding to Slc22a6 underscores a potential role in drug-target interactions, particularly in the context of transport-related pathways.",
